WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.716 --> 00:00:11.678
This lab holds up to three named cultural contexts side by side, with one week's concepts as the lens. It keeps differences visible and makes you name the limits.

2
00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:22.681
Read the comparison contract first. Its rules protect intellectual honesty and keep Indigenous, regional, racial, religious, and national differences from collapsing into one another.

3
00:00:22.681 --> 00:00:32.765
Choose a week for the lens. The Use the concepts from selector changes which questions the lab asks. Change the week and watch the questions change.

4
00:00:32.765 --> 00:00:40.861
Select up to three named contexts. Pick contexts that let you test the week's concepts against real, distinct cases.

5
00:00:40.861 --> 00:00:51.864
Work the comparison and write your notes. Culture can shape meaning and practice, but it never operates alone: law, language, migration, institutions, history, and power also matter. The lab makes you name the limit; it will not write the conclusion for you.

6
00:00:51.864 --> 00:00:59.587
Reset when you want a fresh comparison. Reset comparison clears your selected contexts so you can start again.

7
00:00:59.587 --> 00:01:09.048
Follow the assessment bridge at the end. It connects lab thinking to the course assessments. The official instructions stay on Blackboard.

8
00:01:09.048 --> 00:01:14.242
Nothing in the lab is scored. It exists to sharpen your written work.

9
00:01:14.242 --> 00:01:20.173
Where next: Carry one difference that must remain into the Course Synthesis page.
